{"causes": ["The outbreak of the Peloponnesian War in 431 BCE necessitated Spartan military leadership across land and sea.", "The Mytilenean Revolt against Athens in 428 BCE provided a strategic opportunity for Sparta to challenge Athenian dominance in the Aegean.", "Sparta's traditional focus on land warfare meant a relative lack of experienced naval commanders capable of matching Athenian expertise."], "effects": ["Alcidas's ineffective command reinforced Athenian naval supremacy in the Aegean during the early Peloponnesian War.", "His failure to relieve Mytilene contributed directly to the city's fall and subsequent harsh Athenian reprisal.", "The strategic shortcomings demonstrated by Alcidas spurred later Spartan efforts to develop a more effective and aggressive naval force."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "peloponnesian-war", "sourceName": "Peloponnesian War", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "alcidas", "targetName": "Alcidas", "context": "Alcidas served as a Spartan navarch during the early phases of the Peloponnesian War from 428-427 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "alcidas", "sourceName": "Alcidas", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "mytilenean-revolt", "targetName": "Mytilenean Revolt", "context": "Alcidas led the Peloponnesian fleet attempting to relieve Mytilene during its revolt against Athens in 428-427 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "alcidas", "sourceName": "Alcidas", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "aegean-sea", "targetName": "Aegean Sea", "context": "Alcidas's primary area of command and operations was the Aegean Sea during his expedition to Mytilene."}, {"sourceSlug": "paches", "sourceName": "Paches", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "alcidas", "targetName": "Alcidas", "context": "The Athenian commander Paches's swift actions in blockading Mytilene and pursuing Alcidas's fleet forced the Spartan navarch's retreat."}, {"sourceSlug": "alcidas", "sourceName": "Alcidas", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "spartan-naval-strategy", "targetName": "Spartan Naval Strategy", "context": "Alcidas's failures highlighted critical weaknesses in early Spartan naval strategy, prompting future adaptations and improvements."}], "places": [{"name": "Mytilene, Lesbos", "role": "Primary objective of naval expedition"}, {"name": "Aegean Sea, Greece", "role": "Main theater of operations"}, {"name": "Corcyra, Greece", "role": "Site of later naval engagement"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Alcidas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "alcidas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3271807", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcidas"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Alcidas's command revealed critical deficiencies in early Spartan naval strategy and execution, directly contributing to Athens' continued maritime dominance in the early Peloponnesian War and shaping the conflict's initial phase. His failures prompted important lessons that informed subsequent Spartan naval development.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-12T09:39:10Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27407391651", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Alcidas was a Spartan navarch during the Peloponnesian War.", "newValue": "Alcidas, a Spartan navarch, held a critical, if ultimately unsuccessful, command during the early stages of the Peloponnesian War, specifically from 428 to 427 BCE. Tasked with supporting the Mytilenean Revolt against Athens, his leadership in the Aegean Sea exposed significant deficiencies in Spartan naval strategy and execution. His actions directly influenced the trajectory of the war's initial phase, underscoring Athens' formidable maritime power and compelling Sparta to later re-evaluate its approach to naval warfare.

Alcidas's most notable engagement began in 428 BCE when he led a Peloponnesian fleet of 42 ships to relieve Mytilene, which was under Athenian siege. His cautious and indecisive approach, however, proved disastrous. Instead of making a swift dash to Mytilene, Alcidas lingered, allowing the Athenian commander Paches to complete the circumvallation of the city. Upon learning of Mytilene's fall in 427 BCE, Alcidas's fleet, rather than engaging the Athenians, retreated, committing atrocities against Greek merchant vessels and coastal towns. His subsequent flight across the Aegean, pursued by Paches, culminated in his return to the Peloponnese without having achieved any strategic objective, only to later participate in the indecisive Battle of Corcyra in 427 BCE.

